===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who practices [[chiromancy]]; a [[palm]] [[reader]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Virgilio Malvezzi|authorlink=Virgilio Malvezzi|author2=[[w:Henry Carey, 2nd Earl of Monmouth|Henry Carey, Baron Carey of Leppington]], transl.|chapter=[[w:Lucius Tarquinius Superbus|Tarquin]] Makes Peace with the [[w:Aequi|Equi]], Renewes His League with the Tuscans, and Betaking Himselfe to Citie Affairs, will Finish the Temple of Jupiter Tarpeius|title=Romvlvs and Tarqvin: First Written in Italian by the Marques Virgilio Malvezzi; and now Taught English by H: Ld. Cary of Lepingtō|edition=2nd|location=London|publisher=Printed by I. H. for Iohn Benton, and are to be sould at his shopp under [[w:St Dunstan-in-the-West|St Dunstons Church]] [[w:Fleet Street|Fleet street]]|year=1638|page=225|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=38FjAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA225|oclc=54152833|passage=The ſcumme of the people partake of the '''Chyromancer'''; they will ſee the hand, to judge the heart: but how many are there who preſent themſelves before God with hands of gold, and hearts of clay?}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Richard Sanders|chapter=What Physiognomie is, and what of It is Requisite for the Chiromancer to Know|title=Physiognomie, and Chiromancie, Metoposcopie, the Symmetrical Proportions and Signal Moles of the Body, Fully and Accurately Handled; with Their Natural-predictive Significations. The Subject of Dreams; Divinative, Steganographical, and Lullian Sciences. Whereunto is Added the Art of Memorie|location=London|publisher=Printed by R. White, for Nathaniel Brooke, at the sign of the angel in [[w:Cornhill, London|Cornhil]] near the [[w:Royal Exchange, London|Royal Exchange]]|year=1653|page=143|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hGxjAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA143|oclc=702365936|passage={{w|Hippocrates}} Prince of Phyſick, ſayes, that the Phyſician cannot be accompliſhed in his Art, if he have not the knowledge of ''Aſtrologie''; ſo I may ſay that the '''''Chiromancer''''' is not perfect nor accompliſhed if he have not ''Phyſiognomie'', which is ''Scienta quâ natura hominum ex aſpectu corporis judicatur'', {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|author=F. T. M‘Dougall|chapter=Hand, Regions of the|editor=[[w:Robert Bentley Todd|Robert B[entley] Todd]]|title=The Cyclopædia of Anatomy and Physiology|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman, Brown, Green, Longmans, & Roberts]]|year=1836–1839|volume=II (DIA–INS)|page=524|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TzoAAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA524|oclc=14856472|passage=There are many other inconstant folds, or markings of the skin, in this region [of the hand], which, to the surgeon, are of little import, but which present a book of mystic lore to the gipsy and the '''cheiromancer''', wherein (when opened by the necessary charms) they discern the future destinies of all that seek to be enlightened by them.}}
#* {{quote-book|editor=Albany Poyntz|chapter=Fortune-tellers and Chiromancy|title=A World of Wonders, with Anecdotes and Opinions Concerning Popular Superstitions|location=London|publisher=[[w:Richard Bentley (publisher)|Richard Bentley]], {{w|New Burlington Street}}, publisher in ordinary to Her Majesty|year=1845|pages=126–127|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Lj9o9nxX13QC&pg=PA126|oclc=29297062|passage='''Chiromancers''' divide the hand into several regions, each presided by a planet. The thumb belongs to Venus, the index to Jupiter, the middle finger to Saturn, the annulary to the Sun, the auricular to Mercury, the centre of the hand to Mars, the remainder to the Moon. The direction of the line of life is still undecided by '''chiromancers'''; some placing it between the thumb and index, traversing the centre of the palm; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Graeme Tytler|chapter=[[w:Johann Kaspar Lavater|Lavater]] and the ''Physiognomische Fragmente''|title=Physiognomy in the European Novel: Faces and Fortunes|location=Princeton, N.J.|publisher={{w|Princeton University Press}}|year=1982|page=43|isbn=978-0-691-06491-8|passage=It was considered blasphemous to forestall Providence, as most '''chiromancers''' and metoposcopists did, by predicting future events in everyday life from sundry bodily signs; and this accounts for the widespread belief throughout those centuries that physiognomy was a kind of black magic.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Giovan[ni] Francesco Straparola|authorlink=Giovanni Straparola|author2=W. G. Waters, transl.|chapter=King Galafro's Vain Precautions|editor=Donald Beecher|title=[[w:The Facetious Nights of Straparola|The Pleasant Nights]]|series=The Lorenzo da Ponte Italian Library|location=Toronto|publisher={{w|University of Toronto Press}}|year=2012|volume=2|page=264|isbn=978-1-4426-4427-4|passage=Now this '''chiromancer''', when he understood the king's wish, took hold of his hand and examined with the greatest care all the lines he could trace there. After considering them diligently, one and all, he stood silent and his face grew pale.}}

